index 8e26a1a3d19b3f60c5ca8dd5acb13ed98c2895c9..3394dde6e3bb98975459866156e827abbacb2791 100644
--- a/CONTRACT.md
+++ b/CONTRACT.md
@@ -338,6 +338,40 @@ only because the definition cannot be named directly. Those lower to an ordinary
 `core`'s `panic_impl` (the `#[panic_handler]`) is the case that matters; the allocator shims have
 the same shape.
 
+### `#[track_caller]`
+
+A function rustc says `requires_caller_location` takes the caller's `Location` as one trailing
+parameter, `$loc`; a call to it appends the call's location, or, inside another tracked function,
+that function's own `$loc`, so a chain reports its outermost untracked caller as native code does.
+A `fn` pointer call cannot pass the location, so a pointer to a tracked function names the
+`ReifyShim` rustc builds (`Instance::resolve_for_fn_ptr`), which reports the function's own
+definition. A function item handed over as a value is called the same way, with its arguments
+alone, so it names the same shim on both lanes: a host callback (`callback0`.., `try_host`, the
+`view!` holes; v1 `abi.rs::closure_thunk`, `lower/js/host.rs::closure_item`) and a keyed-list
+collection, key or row (`jir/encode.rs::callable_body`). The collector instantiates a reify shim
+only where MIR coerces to a pointer, so the unit asks for it (`CguCx::require_reified`), and the
+baseline text naming it is what keeps it reachable for the JIR lane. A key extractor's purity is
+proven on the tracked function the shim calls, because the effects summary claims nothing for a
+call that passes a trailing location (`template.rs::key_effects`); a key that reads its location
+is refused as it was before. A shim shares its definition and type with the function it wraps, so
+the producer keys a shim's `ItemId` by the whole instance: the link step's per-item facts (the
+item index a tracked body consults to decide whether a callee takes its `$loc`, the flat ABI plan)
+never conflate the two.
+
+Limits, both present before the rules above:
+
+- A tracked function whose body makes any *virtual* call (to a tracked trait method or not, a
+  `&dyn Fn` callback included) is declined by the JIR lowering: JIR-S records a call's location as
+  `None` both for "takes none" and for "forward this body's `$loc`", the lowering tells the two
+  apart from the callee's item-index entry, and a virtual callee has none. The baseline text is
+  right, but a release profile refuses the fallback when the body owes cleanup, and the program
+  does not compile. The fix is for the producer to say "forward" explicitly in the call (a
+  JIR-S/JIR-R schema change).
+- rustc's MIR inliner (`JSC_RUST_MIR_INLINE=on`) can place a tracked call inside a body it inlined;
+  rustc then walks the inlined scopes (`mir::Body::caller_location_span`), the producer spells the
+  call's own span on both lanes. Release profiles lower user code from MIR captured before
+  inlining and are right; cleanup-off modes report a location inside the inlined callee.
+
 ### `#[panic_handler]` crates must not format
 
 A `#![no_std]` crate's panic handler may read `PanicInfo::location()` — the `Location` arrives
